Because I'm pretty much entirely at Apple's mercy here. Their license
agreements disallow any tinkering except on their terms. I don't own an
iPhone (or any other mobile phone, for that matter), but even if I did,
I couldn't use it to develop a NetHack variant unless I jumped through
whatever hoops Apple wanted me to jump through (including paying money,
buying a particular brand of microcomputer, and so on). Likewise, any
end user would have to pay Apple the same developer fee if they wanted
to be able to patch the game themselves. Elsewhere in your post, you
talk about the difference between developers and end users. I don't see
why there should be one, especially for single-player games; modding
games is a time-honoured tradition.

Also, Apple's restrictions on the app store are probably not to do with
security, but with maintaining a revenue stream. They're trying to
ensure that no program running on the iPhone can make anyone money
unless Apple get a cut of it (this is the reason I most commonly hear of
for an app being rejected). They ban the ability to easily upload code
for the same reason; if people could run what they liked on their
phones, they could buy a paid app separately from them, and then run it.

